---
title: Turmeric-Coconut Braised Chicken Thighs
course: main
cuisine: southeast-asian
servings: 2
time_active: 15
time_total: 40
leftovers: great
tags: [one-pot]
---

Pat @chicken thighs{4}(bone-in, skin-on, about 1 1/4 lb) very dry. Season all over with @salt{3/4%tsp} and @black pepper{1/4%tsp}.

Heat @neutral oil{1%tbsp} in a 3-quart #dutch oven{} over medium-high. Sear the thighs skin-side down until deep golden, ~{4-5%minutes}. Flip and brown ~{2%minutes} more. Move to a plate; they won't be cooked through.

Lower the heat to medium and spoon off all but about 1 tbsp of fat. Add @fresh ginger{1%tbsp}(peeled and finely grated), @ground turmeric{1%tsp}, and @?garlic{1%clove}(minced). Stir until fragrant, ~{30%seconds}, without letting the turmeric burn.

Pour in @coconut milk{1%can}(13.5 oz, full-fat, shaken) and @water{1/2%cup}(or low-sodium chicken stock), scraping up the browned bits. Bring to a gentle simmer and season with @salt{1/4%tsp} and more pepper.

Return the thighs skin-side up, keeping the skin above the liquid. Cover with the lid ajar and simmer gently ~{25%minutes}.

Squeeze in half of @lime{1}, then taste and add salt or more lime. Sprinkle with @cilantro{2%tbsp}(chopped, or parsley) and a pinch of @?red pepper flakes{}.

Serve over @?jasmine rice{} with the rest of the lime in wedges.

> If the sauce reduces too much, add a splash of water; if it's loose at the end, simmer uncovered 2 to 3 minutes. Keeps 3 days in the fridge, and the flavor deepens overnight.
